# Invention No. 13 in A minor, BWV 784 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: restless and lyrical at once, built on a broken-chord
# subject: half a bar of sixteenths (after a sixteenth rest, an A minor
# arpeggio with a turn) and half a bar of leaping eighths. The two hands
# trade it every half bar, so the music never stops moving. It wants a
# flowing, singing tempo, with weight on the harmony that changes each
# half bar.
#
# Time signature: 4/4, 25 bars in the Mutopia score and in the MIDI;
# the first note (the bass A) is on the downbeat of bar 1.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 76, 100 quarters, about 1:19.
# Track times (approximate): Gustav Leonhardt (harpsichord, Apple Music
# reissue) 1:19, about 76; Andras Schiff (ECM, clavichord) 1:22, about
# 73; Angela Hewitt (Hyperion) 1:10, about 86; Glenn Gould (Sony, 1964)
# 0:45, about 133, a fast, detached outlier not followed. 76 is
# Leonhardt's tempo, between Schiff and Hewitt.
#
# Structure (read from the Mutopia score and checked against the MIDI;
# the last return of the subject at 18, the coda and the Neapolitan in
# bar 23 follow the teoria.com analysis (Jose Rodriguez Alvira); the
# division into two halves with two subject statements in the first
# follows the Japanese Piano Encyclopedia (PTNA) entry):
#   1-2    Subject in the top on each first half bar, in the bass on
#          each second half (the two hands alternate), A minor.
#   3-6    Sequence by falling fifths on the inverted figure, cadence in
#          C major at bar 6, beat 3.
#   7-8    Subject in C major, alternating as in bars 1-2.
#   9-12   Sequence by falling fifths, toward E minor.
#   13     Cadence in E minor (end of the first half).
#   14-17  Second half: diminished-seventh arpeggios in the top over
#          leaping bass eighths, with a bass echo at each bar end,
#          falling by step (D minor, C, toward A minor).
#   18     Subject returns in A minor, top, then bass (its last full
#          return, as in bar 1).
#   19-21  Sequence over a falling bass, on the dominant (E) in 20.
#   22     The hands cross: the bass plays its arpeggio above the top,
#          then the top has the subject once more (second half).
#   23-25  Coda: a rising chromatic bass, a Neapolitan B-flat (23),
#          final cadence in A minor with a fermata, no Picardy third.

tempo 76

voice mid 0          # two voices only: there is no middle voice to set back

# No lead marks in bars 1-2, 7-8 and 18: the subject changes hands every
# half bar there, and a lead mark lasts at least a bar, so it would lift
# the wrong voice half the time. Even balance is right. Bar 22 has the
# hands crossed, so it is left alone too.

dyn 1 mf             # bars 1-6: exposition, up to the C major cadence
dyn 7 mp             # bars 7-8: the subject again in C major, an echo
dyn 9 mf             # bars 9-13: sequence to the E minor cadence
ramp 14-17 f         # bars 14-17: the diminished-seventh descent, the
                     # one long build, into the return
                     # (bars 18-25 stay at f to the end)
lead 23 bottom       # bar 23: the rising chromatic bass into the
                     # Neapolitan B-flat

rit 1.5 0.7          # from the middle of bar 24 into the final fermata
